CRITERION 1 (no secret in any argv, no global export): PASS
 Attack: every route a secret could take to an argv or to a non-tools.py environment.
 - strace -f -v -e trace=execve,execveat of a full real-container run (oracle A4) and of my own fake-docker run on a Go tree
   (covers go env / go mod download): smoketest/codex/trace_check.py -> "ok 557 557 2" (557 execs, 2 carry the secret in their
   environment, both tools.py: copy and keep); no argv carries it. Oracle check "A4 no argv carries a secret..." ok.
 - Mutant: pys() back to "env CD_SECRETS=..." -> unit suite FAILS 3 (A4-1 x3). Mutant killed.
 - Static: only SECRETS_JSON="" / SECRETS_JSON="$r1" / the pys prefix assignment; export lines carry CD_* paths only.
 - Here-strings: bash 5.2.21 spills a here-string to a 0600 tmp file only at >= 65536 bytes (probed 3000..70000); a real auth
   file's secret list is far smaller (known backlog item, not an item-1 scope issue).
 - E2BIG (over 128 KiB env string) fails closed (container-error), not open.
 Observation O1 (not a FAIL, see below): an exported SECRETS_JSON in the CALLER's environment survives bash's export attribute.
 Repro: env SECRETS_JSON=x strace ... bash swarm/codex-check.sh T 1 -> trace_check reports the secret in the environment of
 mktemp, git, docker, cat, ... (same for pre-exported res / r1). Needs a same-named exported variable supplied by the caller;
 /proc/<pid>/environ is owner-only (0400), unlike cmdline. Fix would be declare +x / unset of those names at the top.

